n AI  is crowded out by Senate testimonies or  something and so and I think it speaks  to the public's great interest in this  topic that I don't think the public is  misplaced in their interest  an AI of course has huge implications  across almost everything or everything  and so where everything includes things  like smart cars banking medicine law  advanced manufacturing disruption of the  labor force and the nature of work  itself certainly over the course of my  own scientific career I think there few  technologies have come along that has  captured the public's attention with the  equivalent disruptive potential as  artificial intelligence that disruption  always comes with significant societal  challenges and opportunities and as part  of our new AI program we are  establishing a new initiative called AI  in society that will bring together some  of the world's leading thinkers in this  area social scientists humanists and  scientists to engage in long term deeply  collaborative discussions with each  other with governments and with civil  society indeed I think today's address  by darrone can be regarded as a launch  of our program in this area see far  senior fellow Daron Acemoglu has been  examining some of these issues and will  be sharing them with us all today but  first let me say a few words about David  dodge actually David is somebody who  truly needs no introduction  especially in this town he is a giant  both little literally and figuratively  and and I think would be redundant for  me to say more about David in Ottawa  this lecture series is named after David  to recognize his enormous legacy of  contributions both the CFR and to the  country and as a supporter and board  member and board chair here at CFR  David's interest in CFR actually I think  began with one of our other programs on  the importance of the early years and  children and now call child and brain  development and I think again it  typifies I think what is really the best  about 